  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nDiffluent southwest flow aloft has begun in the Northern \nPlains. An upper level low is developing off the Rocky Mountains\nin Montana. At the surface, a large low currently sits in far \nsoutheastern Montana and slowly moving into North Dakota. Ahead \nof this low stagnate and moist air sits across western North \nDakota, leading to a large area of dense fog. We have issued a \nDense Fog Advisory for western and south central ND until the \nlate morning. On the backside of the low a large area of precip \nis across Montana, currently falling as snow. \n\nThis afternoon when the surface low moves into central and\neastern North Dakota, the pressure gradient on the backside of\nthe low will create very breezy winds across the southwest and\nJames River Valley. A Wind Advisory and High Wind Warning are \nstill in effect through this evening for the southwest and south\ncentral. South Dakota will see the higher wind speeds being \nfarther from the low center and in a more tighter pressure \ngradient, so the High Wind Warning is still only for Bowman \nCounty. Confidence is low in high wind warning winds to expand \nelsewhere. Today's highs will be in the 40s to 60s so expect \nrain only to fall this afternoon and evening. As the sun sets \nsnow may be seen falling in northwestern and northern parts of \nthe state but no snow accumulations are expected, especially \nwith rain preceding the snow. This system is expected to fully \npull out of the state by late Thursday morning, leading to a \nquiet and much cooler weather day. The backside of the system is\nwrapping in cold air, which will lead to highs in the 30s.\n\nThe weekend system has changed some, as much less snow\naccumulations are looking possible. Dynamically the system \nhasn't change. Still a deep trough that will kick out a low \npressure system off the Colorado Rocky Mountains and pass \nthrough South Dakota. Diffluent southwest flow and the surface \nlow will start to impact the state Friday morning with a \nrain/snow mix in the west and rain elsewhere. As surface \ntemperatures warm to the 30s and 40s, mostly all rain will \nfall, until the overnight when it will likely switch to snow \nacross the west and north. Most of the QPF will fall north of \nHighway 2. If any snow accumulations happen, it will be near the\nCanadian border and probably less than 2 inches. The speed of \nthe system looks like it has sped up and shifted north, leaving \nthe southern half of the state dry-slotted most of the time. By\nSaturday afternoon the system will be gone, but some linger snow\nmay occur in the Turtle Mountains through the evening. As with \nany low pressure, winds will be westerly and breezy, but sub-\nadvisory level this time. Temperatures will then remain in the \nupper 20s north to mid 30s south as a surface high moves across \nsouthern Canada late this weekend and into Monday. Beyond Monday\nmodels disagree in the flow pattern, but both solutions look \nlike below or at normal (38 degrees) temperatures.",
